# 99-serial-logger.rules — start a logger for every USB serial device.
#
# This is the half of the mechanism that decides WHEN serial-logger@.service
# runs. udev sees the device appear, tags it, and names the service instance
# after the kernel's own name for the node. No polling, no hardcoded path,
# no service sitting in a retry loop waiting for hardware to show up.
#
# Why "99-": udev processes rules files in lexical order across all its
# directories, and the convention is that low numbers set things up
# (persistent names, permissions) and high numbers act on the result. Ours
# acts, so it goes late.
#
# THE MATCH, term by term:
#
#   SUBSYSTEM=="tty"
#       The device we are being asked about is a serial port. Note the
#       singular: SUBSYSTEM is the device's OWN subsystem.
#
#   SUBSYSTEMS=="usb"
#       Plural, and this is the load-bearing one. SUBSYSTEMS walks UP the
#       device's parent chain and matches if ANY ancestor is a USB device.
#       That is what separates a USB adapter's port from the Pi's own
#       on-board UART: ttyS0 and ttyAMA0 hang off the SoC's platform bus,
#       have no USB ancestor, and so are never matched. Without this term
#       we would cheerfully start a logger on the serial console and record
#       the boot messages into themselves.
#
#       Matching by ancestry rather than by driver is also why this needs no
#       maintenance: ftdi_sio, cp210x, ch341, cdc_acm and whatever ships next
#       year all produce a tty under a USB parent. The deployed script only
#       knew about /dev/ttyUSB*, which is why plugging in a CDC-ACM device
#       (a Pico Debugprobe — /dev/ttyACM0) broke it.
#
# THE ACTIONS:
#
#   TAG+="systemd"
#       Ask udev to expose this device to systemd as a .device unit. Without
#       the tag there is no dev-ttyACM0.device for the service's BindsTo= to
#       attach to, and SYSTEMD_WANTS is ignored.
#
#   ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}+="serial-logger@%k.service"
#       %k is the kernel name of the device — "ttyACM0", "ttyUSB0". systemd
#       reads this property off the device unit and starts the named service
#       as if the device unit had Wants= on it. "+=" appends, so we coexist
#       with any other rule wanting its own service on the same device.
#
# Plug in two adapters and you get two independent instances, each with its
# own log file. The single-global-PORT design could not do that at all.

SUBSYSTEM=="tty", SUBSYSTEMS=="usb", TAG+="systemd", \
    ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}+="serial-logger@%k.service"
